WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
If part of the brain breaks, how do we know its job?

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.940
A patient named Leborgne could hardly speak, yet much of his understanding survived.

3
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.520
When he died, Broca found damage on the left frontal lobe.

4
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.700
Such damage is a lesion, and studying what fails is a lesion study.

5
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:23.893
Lesion evidence has two sources.

6
00:00:25.253 --> 00:00:29.393
In patient studies, a stroke or operation damages part of the brain.

7
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:36.193
In animal studies, researchers cut a route or destroy a neuron cluster, a nucleus,

8
00:00:36.193 --> 00:00:37.453
with electric current.

9
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:41.253
Either way, compare what fails with what survives.

10
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:45.117
Next, follow the broken route toward the body.

11
00:00:46.537 --> 00:00:49.097
A lesion removes signals from the fibres it cuts.

12
00:00:50.457 --> 00:00:52.597
Count how many midline crossings lie ahead.

13
00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:55.277
Each crossing flips the side.

14
00:00:56.537 --> 00:00:58.577
An odd count affects the opposite side.

15
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:01.577
An even count keeps the same side.

16
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.200
Try that rule on movement.

17
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.440
A stroke deep in the left hemisphere lies above one midline crossing.

18
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.940
One is odd, so it weakens the right face, arm, and leg.

19
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.840
Now cut the right half of the neck spinal cord, below that crossing.

20
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.280
Which side becomes weak?

21
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:28.213
The right arm and leg.

22
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:34.373
The pathway already crossed above the neck, so zero crossings remain below the cut.

23
00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:37.513
Zero is even, so the same side.

24
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:43.553
Lesions on opposite sides weaken the same limbs because both break one chain.

25
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:49.013
So a deficit shows a region is a needed link, not that it works alone.

26
00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:52.017
That is why lesions can mislead.

27
00:01:53.397 --> 00:01:56.877
Destroying one region also cuts fibres passing through it.

28
00:01:57.957 --> 00:01:59.817
And the region may be only a relay.

29
00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:05.717
Real strokes often break several stretches, and different lesions can give one

30
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:11.277
pattern. Researchers guard against this by testing spared skills, comparing

31
00:02:11.277 --> 00:02:13.217
patients, and adding other tests.

32
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.560
Used carefully, lesions unlocked major findings.

33
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.460
In 1957, William Scoville and Brenda Milner described patient H.M.

34
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.820
after his operation.

35
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.840
He could almost never form lasting new declarative memories, the kind you can tell

36
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.740
someone about. Yet practice improved his drawing, so another system kept a record.

37
00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:40.700
Memory is several abilities.

38
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:46.373
The suprachiasmatic nucleus sits just above where the optic nerves meet.

39
00:02:47.693 --> 00:02:53.733
In 1972, Friedrich Stephan and Irving Zucker destroyed it on both sides in albino

40
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:58.413
rats. The rats still drank, but no longer on a daily schedule.

41
00:02:59.713 --> 00:03:03.773
That showed the nucleus is needed for timing, not that it generates time.

42
00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:10.427
In 2002, Olausson and colleagues studied a patient without the large, fast touch

43
00:03:10.427 --> 00:03:17.207
fibres. Stimulating slow C-tactile fibres on hairy skin still gave a faint, pleasant

44
00:03:17.207 --> 00:03:22.947
sensation. That showed what those fibres can do without the large ones, not that

45
00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:27.127
they are needed. Each case compares what broke with what survived.
